{
  "dom_id": "step-q18500",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find the $y$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x$ that is closest to the point $(1,4).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,4)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}-8y+16 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}-8y+17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =y+{y}^{2}-8y+17 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-7y+17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(y)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y)=2y-7 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(y)=2>0.$ Therefore, $y=\\frac{7}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $y=\\frac{7}{2}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
